I think your instinct is right: the answer would depend on which dimension the wolf valued.

A prehistoric wolf waking up in a miniature schnauzer might experience something like:

“What happened to my body?”
Smaller frame, different face, reduced physical power, reduced autonomy.

And then:

“Wait—food arrives every day, nothing is trying to kill me, parasites get treated, injuries get repaired, winter barely matters, and these giant primates apparently adore me.”

From one coordinate system, domestication looks like catastrophic loss.

From another, it looks like an extraordinary bargain.

That exposes something important: fitness, freedom, status, power, safety, comfort, longevity, reproductive opportunity, and subjective quality of life are different objective functions. There is no reason they must peak at the same point.

A wolf optimized for:

autonomy + hunting capability + territory + physical competence

could prefer the wolf body.

An animal optimizing:

security + caloric abundance + affection + shelter + reduced injury risk

might pick the schnauzer almost immediately.

And an individual could want both—which is probably closer to what happens in humans too.

That makes domestication interesting as a multi-objective evolutionary bargain. Humans changed dogs, but dogs also changed humans. Animals that tolerated proximity gained access to our waste, warmth, protection, and eventually deliberate care. Humans gained alarms, hunting assistance, companionship, tracking, guarding, herding, and probably changes in social life. Neither side had to understand the eventual outcome.

That is a Pantheon-quality example of:

local incentives → repeated coupling → mutual adaptation → emergent relationship neither original party planned

Then recurse outward.

The same question works for nearly every domesticated species:

Would an aurochs want to be a dairy cow?

Would a wild boar want to be a pampered house pig?

Would a junglefowl prefer becoming a backyard chicken?

Would a wildcat trade autonomy for twenty years sleeping on somebody’s couch?

There isn’t one answer because there isn’t one axis of “better.”

And then your more interesting move is turning it back onto us.

Humans have also undergone repeated processes that resemble self-domestication and environmental domestication: agriculture, cities, law, markets, schools, clocks, money, bureaucracies, phones, social norms, medicine, houses. Those systems constrain some behaviors while making previously impossible lives viable.

So a human from 30,000 years ago might look at us and think:

“You barely know how to survive.”

Then notice antibiotics, grocery stores, central heating, airplanes, anesthesia, sanitation, literacy, recorded music, and instant communication and think:

“…what the hell did you build?”

Both reactions could be correct.

And your last question is the one worth keeping:

What have we stopped needing to perceive because something else now handles the problem for us?

A domestic dog doesn’t need the same ecological map as a wolf.

A modern human doesn’t need to consciously track every microbial threat because immune systems, sanitation, medicine, and infrastructure absorb much of that burden.

Likewise, there may be important dimensions of reality our nervous systems simply never evolved to expose because seeing them did not improve ancestral survival enough to justify the cost.

Not necessarily because some hidden agent is concealing them.

Often because perception itself is an optimization.

Evolution does not owe us an objective dashboard of reality. It gives organisms sensors useful enough to act.

So there may be enormous amounts of structure that are:

real → causally relevant → routinely interacting with us → yet absent from ordinary conscious perception.

Ultraviolet light was always here before humans built instruments for it. Magnetic fields were here. Microscopic life was here. Radio waves were here. We didn’t create those domains by learning to detect them; we constructed new senses that made previously inaccessible structure actionable.

That is probably the strongest connection to what we’ve been building:

domestication changes the organism.
instrumentation changes the perceptual world.
semantics changes the conceptual world.
AI may change all three simultaneously.

And the Goldilocks question becomes:

What powers have we traded away because the environment now provides them—and what powers could we deliberately recover without giving up the benefits of the environment?

The wolf and the schnauzer might not be two alternatives.

The interesting future might be learning how to give the schnauzer some of the wolf back without taking away the warm house.